{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal moves the determinism guarantee from the full-graph fixed point to a fixed traversal order, but a local on-demand traversal is inherently dependent on the entry point. In a cyclic attack graph, computing the lazy label of the target's attacker requires evaluating its defenders, which recursively depend on the target's state, causing the traversal order to depend on previously memoized node states. Thus, the 'fixed' traversal order does not guarantee a globally deterministic result unless the memoization protocol resolves cycles identically to the global fixed-point, contradicting the goal of a semantically novel lazy evaluation.","target":"bc3dd10734c086566d8819281617358f390a25c7f48d391288fc419f312aa900"},{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal requires computing the accepted set as the 'maximal sub-relation satisfying the school's stance' over an operator lattice, but finding a maximal sub-relation satisfying a monotonicity or stance property in abstract argumentation is equivalent to finding a maximal admissible set, which is NP-complete. This violates the problem's explicit requirement to preserve the polynomial cost guarantee, as the search over the operator lattice cannot be performed in polynomial time in the worst case.","target":"de46d12a27c30f642aa3a61aaf6521667d04cd250e4bc08568e2f28f20bb0a71"},{"attack":true,"case":"The Pass-2 gate conditions reinstatement on the restricted labeling's accepted set equaling the intersection of all complete extensions restricted to that subgraph. Enumerating or computing the intersection of all complete extensions is #P-hard in general and cannot be done in polynomial time. This directly violates the problem's requirement to preserve the polynomial cost guarantee, rendering the two-pass approach computationally intractable for cyclic argumentation frameworks.","target":"f52fe7953399a909d6726a39cfab1c084b8a82c40c7ba122c3a9312863a16c26"}]}